                                                                                <div class="card-item-wrapper ficha-extra hidden h-full" data-categorias="[&quot;monumentos&quot;]">
                        <a href="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/CentroHistorico/lugares/monumento-madre" class="group bg-[#F4F0E9] rounded-[1.5rem] shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-300 overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 flex flex-col h-full">
    <div class="relative aspect-[4/3] overflow-hidden rounded-t-[1.5rem]">
        
        <img src="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/images/Centro_Historico/monumentos/MonumentoMadre.jpg"
             data-ms-field="lugares.monumento-madre.image"             class="w-full h-full object-cover group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500"
            alt="Monument to the Mother">
    </div>
    
    <div class="p-5 flex flex-col flex-grow justify-between gap-2">
        <div>
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-1 para que el título no salte a más de una línea si es largo -->
            <h3 class="text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-sm md:text-base tracking-wider uppercase fuente-titulos line-clamp-1">
                
                Monument to the Mother
            </h3>
            
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-3 limita el texto a 3 líneas y pone los "..." -->
            <p class="text-gray-600 text-xs md:text-sm font-medium font-sans mt-1.5 leading-relaxed line-clamp-3">
                
                The Monument to the Mother, inaugurated on May 10, 1949, on Insurgentes Norte Avenue, was created by sculptor Luis Ortiz Monasterio and architect José Villagrán García as a tribute to motherhood and the family. Its origin dates back to 1922, when Mother&#039;s Day was instituted in Mexico thanks to Rafael Alducín and José Vasconcelos. The sculptural group, in Art Deco style with pre-Hispanic influence, includes figures representing the mother, education, and fertility. After suffering serious damage in the earthquake of September 19, 2017, the monument was restored and reinaugurated in 2018 using original materials.
            </p>
        </div>
        
        <span class="text-[#B19657] group-hover:text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-[10px] md:text-xs tracking-wider uppercase flex items-center gap-1 transition-colors mt-2">
           LEARN MORE<span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 duration-300">→</span>
        </span>
    </div>
</a>                    </div>
                                                                                <div class="card-item-wrapper ficha-extra hidden h-full" data-categorias="[&quot;monumentos&quot;]">
                        <a href="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/CentroHistorico/lugares/altar-patria" class="group bg-[#F4F0E9] rounded-[1.5rem] shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-300 overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 flex flex-col h-full">
    <div class="relative aspect-[4/3] overflow-hidden rounded-t-[1.5rem]">
        
        <img src="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/images/Centro_Historico/lugares/AltarPatria.png"
             data-ms-field="lugares.altar-patria.image"             class="w-full h-full object-cover group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500"
            alt="Altar to the Homeland">
    </div>
    
    <div class="p-5 flex flex-col flex-grow justify-between gap-2">
        <div>
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-1 para que el título no salte a más de una línea si es largo -->
            <h3 class="text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-sm md:text-base tracking-wider uppercase fuente-titulos line-clamp-1">
                
                Altar to the Homeland
            </h3>
            
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-3 limita el texto a 3 líneas y pone los "..." -->
            <p class="text-gray-600 text-xs md:text-sm font-medium font-sans mt-1.5 leading-relaxed line-clamp-3">
                
                The Altar to the Homeland, popularly known as the obelisk of the heroes, was inaugurated on April 21, 1998, in the roundabout of 16 de Septiembre and Arista streets in the center of Veracruz. This monument replaced an earlier one from 1952 and was created to pay tribute not only to the heroes of the US invasions of 1847 and 1914, but also to the defenders of the French intervention of 1838 and the victims of the bombardment of 1823, events linked to the city&#039;s title of &quot;Four Times Heroic.&quot; The obelisk, covered in black marble and raised on staircases, contains inscriptions referring to each conflict and seeks to concentrate in a single site the memory of those who defended Veracruz against foreign attacks.
            </p>
        </div>
        
        <span class="text-[#B19657] group-hover:text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-[10px] md:text-xs tracking-wider uppercase flex items-center gap-1 transition-colors mt-2">
           LEARN MORE<span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 duration-300">→</span>
        </span>
    </div>
</a>                    </div>
                                                                                <div class="card-item-wrapper ficha-extra hidden h-full" data-categorias="[&quot;monumentos&quot;]">
                        <a href="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/CentroHistorico/lugares/emigrante-libanes" class="group bg-[#F4F0E9] rounded-[1.5rem] shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-300 overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 flex flex-col h-full">
    <div class="relative aspect-[4/3] overflow-hidden rounded-t-[1.5rem]">
        
        <img src="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/images/Centro_Historico/monumentos/libanes.jpg"
             data-ms-field="lugares.emigrante-libanes.image"             class="w-full h-full object-cover group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500"
            alt="Monument to the Lebanese Emigrant">
    </div>
    
    <div class="p-5 flex flex-col flex-grow justify-between gap-2">
        <div>
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-1 para que el título no salte a más de una línea si es largo -->
            <h3 class="text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-sm md:text-base tracking-wider uppercase fuente-titulos line-clamp-1">
                
                Monument to the Lebanese Emigrant
            </h3>
            
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-3 limita el texto a 3 líneas y pone los "..." -->
            <p class="text-gray-600 text-xs md:text-sm font-medium font-sans mt-1.5 leading-relaxed line-clamp-3">
                
                The Monument to the Lebanese Emigrant, located on Manuel Ávila Camacho Boulevard in the port of Veracruz, was erected as a tribute to the Lebanese community that arrived in the city between the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Many immigrants from the then Ottoman Empire arrived at the Veracruz port seeking new commercial opportunities and a better quality of life. Over time, Lebanese families deeply integrated into the economic, cultural, and social life of Veracruz, outstanding in commerce, gastronomy, and business life. This monument symbolises gratitude for their contribution to the development of the port and keeps alive the memory of those who crossed the ocean to settle in Mexico.
            </p>
        </div>
        
        <span class="text-[#B19657] group-hover:text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-[10px] md:text-xs tracking-wider uppercase flex items-center gap-1 transition-colors mt-2">
           LEARN MORE<span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 duration-300">→</span>
        </span>
    </div>
</a>                    </div>
                                                                                <div class="card-item-wrapper ficha-extra hidden h-full" data-categorias="[&quot;monumentos&quot;]">
                        <a href="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/CentroHistorico/lugares/inmigracion-judia" class="group bg-[#F4F0E9] rounded-[1.5rem] shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-300 overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 flex flex-col h-full">
    <div class="relative aspect-[4/3] overflow-hidden rounded-t-[1.5rem]">
        
        <img src="https://descubre.veracruzmunicipio.gob.mx/images/Centro_Historico/monumentos/inmigracion-judia.jpg"
             data-ms-field="lugares.inmigracion-judia.image"             class="w-full h-full object-cover group-hover:scale-105 transition-transform duration-500"
            alt="Jewish Immigration Commemorative Monument">
    </div>
    
    <div class="p-5 flex flex-col flex-grow justify-between gap-2">
        <div>
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-1 para que el título no salte a más de una línea si es largo -->
            <h3 class="text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-sm md:text-base tracking-wider uppercase fuente-titulos line-clamp-1">
                
                Jewish Immigration Commemorative Monument
            </h3>
            
            <!-- Modificado: line-clamp-3 limita el texto a 3 líneas y pone los "..." -->
            <p class="text-gray-600 text-xs md:text-sm font-medium font-sans mt-1.5 leading-relaxed line-clamp-3">
                
                The Jewish Immigration Commemorative Monument recalls the arrival of Jewish families who entered Mexico through the port of Veracruz during different stages of the 20th century. Many of these immigrants were escaping conflicts, persecutions, and economic crises in Europe and the Middle East, finding in Veracruz a gateway to a new life. The Jewish community contributed significantly to the cultural, educational, and commercial growth of the region, while preserving their traditions and customs. The monument represents hope, integration, and historical recognition of those immigrants who made Mexico their new home.
            </p>
        </div>
        
        <span class="text-[#B19657] group-hover:text-[#5C113C] font-bold text-[10px] md:text-xs tracking-wider uppercase flex items-center gap-1 transition-colors mt-2">
           LEARN MORE<span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 duration-300">→</span>
        </span>
    </div>
</a>                    </div>
